                                                        |(^[[33mLIST^[[0m 1 2 3 (^[[33mLIST^[[0m 4 5 6) 7 8 9) ?~
F~R (^[[?~@?                                            |#<LIST> (1 2 3 (4 5 6) 7 8 9)  (7 elements)
                                                        |
                                                        |> car  "1"
                                                        |  cdr  "(2 3 (4 5 6) 7 8 9)"
                                                        |  nth 0  "1"
                                                        |  nth 1  "2"
                                                        |  nth 2  "3"
                                                        |  nth 3  "(4 5 6)"
                                                        |  nth 4  "7"
                                                        |  nth 5  "8"
                                                        |  nth 6  "9"

With the inspector in ncurses:

The contents of the lisp are not strings, therefore strings should not be displayed.
use PRIN1 not PRINC.

A colon may be used to separate the index/name of the slot from the value:

car:   1
cdr:   (2 3 (4 5 6) 7 8 9)
nth 0: 1
nth 1: 2
nth 2: 3
nth 4: (4 5 6)
…


Outside of ncurses (when ncurses windows are not displayed, in the dumb ui REPL), inspect invokes ncurses but doesn't even display an inspector windows.
I think it would be better to stay with the dumb ui when called from the dumb-iu REPL for now.

* Status 2026-08-26

DONE (clautolisp 1.9.19): the ncurses inspector now shows each component as
"LABEL: VALUE" and prints the value with its own PRIN1 string
(INSPECT-COMPONENT-PREVIEW) directly, instead of re-running the ncurses PREVIEW
helper on it — which PRIN1'd the already-printed string again and wrapped every
value in quotes.

TODO: routing — when `inspect' is invoked from the dumb-ui REPL (no ncurses
windows up, not at a debugger stop), keep it in the dumb inspector instead of
bringing up ncurses. Needs tracing how the REPL's inspect reaches the session
UI (the session's UI is the ncurses one under `--debugger-ui ncurses'); the fix
is to pick the inspector surface from the invocation context (REPL vs stop),
not from the session UI. Deferred for a focused change.

